resources.string:
The company profile uses AWS Client VPN with SAML sign-in (auth-federate). OpenVPN Connect and the regular OpenVPN for Android cannot sign in with it, and the official AWS VPN Client exists only for Windows, macOS and Linux. AWSOpenVPN adds what is missing:
• the SAML flow: challenge (CRV1), sign-in page in the browser, local receiver on 127.0.0.1:35001, and a second connection with the SAML response;
• a patched OpenVPN core whose buffers accept the ±10 KB SAML token (the stock core fails with "Key Method #2 write failed");
• the server IP pinned for the whole sign-in (the SAML session belongs to that server);
• per-app VPN, so Tiny Container, Termux, Tryminal and the browsers reach the private network.

Sign-in providers: AWSOpenVPN works with whichever SAML 2.0 identity provider the AWS Client VPN endpoint uses (Microsoft Entra ID, Okta, Google Workspace, OneLogin, JumpCloud, AWS IAM Identity Center and others). So far it has been tested with Microsoft Entra ID; other providers use the same flow but are not tested yet. Profiles with certificates only work as in regular OpenVPN; Active Directory (user name and password) sign-in has not been tested.

AWS officially supports SAML sign-in only in its own client, so a change on the AWS side can break this app without notice.
